# Logging

Use the standard library `logging` package so callers can control verbosity without print spam.

## Module setup

```python
import logging

logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)
logger.addHandler(logging.NullHandler())
```

## Levels

- `debug` — dense internals (matrix nnz, bounds, per-iteration dumps)
- `info` — high-level progress (start/finish of major steps, summaries)
- `warning` — recoverable issues (quality problems, skipped collapses)
- `error` — failures that block the operation

Prefer lazy `%s`-style formatting: `logger.info("Built L: nnz=%d", L.nnz)`.

## Public APIs

- Optional `log: logging.Logger | None = None`; default to the module logger.
- Optional `verbose: bool = False` may bump useful progress to `info` for interactive use; do not replace proper level usage.
- Library code must not configure the root logger or force handlers; leave that to scripts/notebooks.

```python
_log = log or logger
_log.info("MCF: %d iterations, dt=%g", iterations, dt)
_log.debug("System shape: %s", A.shape)
```
